{
  "dom_id": "step-9594",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "The **area** of a shape is a measure of how much space is enclosed by a shape on a plane. Let's consider a square with sides of length of $1\\,\\textrm{in}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14195146/assets/bc59b6e5a60a2f22e6c7fb102eba2f255dd09e115fd04a16050570f9f8b053f4.png)\n\nThe area of this square is equal to ${\\color{red}{1\\,\\textrm{in}^2}},$ which we say in words as \"one square inch.\"\n\nSimilarly, if we have three squares with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm},$ $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ and $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$ respectively, then their respective areas will be \n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2, \\qquad\n\t1\\,\\textrm{m}^2, \\qquad \n\t1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2.\n$$",
